Counting loops:
- one loop from 1 to n: n executions of its body;
- a loop inside a loop, both to n: $n \times n = n^2$;
- an inner loop that runs from 1 to the outer variable i: $1 + 2 + \dots + n = \dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}$, which is still $O(n^2)$;
- a loop whose variable doubles (`i := 1; while i < n do i := i * 2`): about $\log_2 n$ executions, exactly $\lceil \log_2 n \rceil$ here.

Searching and sorting cases:
- linear search: best 1 comparison, worst n;
- binary search on a sorted array: at most $\lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor + 1$ comparisons, since each comparison halves the remaining range;
- insertion sort: already sorted data needs $n - 1$ comparisons (best, $O(n)$) and reversed data needs $\dfrac{n(n-1)}{2}$ (worst, $O(n^2)$).

Binary search in C, an example of the logic (the array must be sorted):

```c
int binary_search(int a[], int n, int target)
{
    int low = 0, high = n - 1;
    while (low <= high) {
        int mid = (low + high) / 2;
        if (a[mid] == target) return mid;
        else if (a[mid] < target) low = mid + 1;
        else high = mid - 1;
    }
    return -1;
}
```
